    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3707": {
      "title": "Research Technician - Vecsey Lab",
      "description": "Category: Research JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States A 12-month, half-time Research Technician position is available in the lab of Dr. Christopher G. Vecsey within the Neuroscience Program at Skidmore College (https://academics.skidmore.edu/blogs/cvecsey/). This position will combine responsibilities for maintaining the lab and also running and analyzing experiments. Job Summary : A 12-month, half-time Research Technician position is available in the lab of Dr. Christopher G. Vecsey within the Neuroscience Program at Skidmore College (https://academics.skidmore.edu/blogs/cvecsey/). This position will combine responsibilities for maintaining the lab and also running and analyzing experiments. Following training, the technician will carry out and analyze the results of behavioral, genetic, electrophysiological, and fluorescent imaging experiments in the fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster , and will assist Dr. Vecsey in training undergraduate students in these techniques. The technician will also collaborate with Dr. Vecsey on the conception of experimental plans and on writing the results for publication, and will also assist with maintaining the fly colonies in the lab and ordering and organizing lab supplies/equipment. Minimum Qualifications: (1) B.A. or B.S. in Neuroscience or related field (e.g., Biology, Biochemistry, Biophysics, Psychology); (2) Excellent verbal and written communication skills; (3) Willingness to learn new techniques through hands-on training and self-motivated learning; and (4) Ability to work independently, in blocks of time that allow for the completion of experiments and other laboratory tasks. Preferred Qualifications : (1) Experience using some or all of the techniques involved in the project (fluorescent imaging of live neurons, electrophysiology, Drosophila genetics, statistical analysis of data, computer coding, especially Python and MATLAB, and using Adobe Illustrator/Photoshop and MS Office, especially Excel); and (2) Interest in staying in the position for two years (possibly for a third, pending reappointment). Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Pay Rate: $30.00/hr. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. No Visa Sponsorship is available for this position EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3712": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Principal Gifts",
      "description": "Category: Advancement JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Associate Director of Principal Gifts manages a portfolio of the College's highest-capacity alumni, parents, and friends, with a primary focus on securing leadership and principal gifts in support of Skidmore College's strategic priorities. Reporting to the Senior Director of Principal and Major Gifts, the position develops and implements individualized cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies for principal gift prospects while collaborating closely with Advancement colleagues, senior administrators, volunteers, and campus partners. The Associate Director serves as a trusted relationship manager for complex donor relationships and supports presidential and volunteer engagement strategies that advance the College's fundraising priorities. Primary Job Duties: Manage a portfolio of principal and leadership gift prospects and donors, typically emphasizing solicitations of $100,000 and above, with a focus on gifts of $500,000 or more. Achieve annual performance benchmarks of at least 75 unique donor/prospect visits and a minimum of $1,000,000 in accepted proposals during the first year while building toward multi-million-dollar fundraising results. Develop and execute individualized qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies for high-capacity alumni, parents, and friends, including current, deferred, blended, and planned gift opportunities. Partner with the Senior Director of Principal and Major Gifts to develop fundraising strategies for the College's most significant prospects, coordinating donor engagement with senior leadership, Trustees, faculty, and volunteers when appropriate. Prepare prospect strategies, briefing materials, visit plans, solicitation proposals, and follow-up communications for presidential, vice presidential, and volunteer-assisted donor engagement. Collaborate with the Senior Director in identifying and advancing prospects for presidential engagement, campaign priorities, and Trustee relationships while maintaining accurate prospect strategies and documentation within the College's CRM. Work collaboratively with colleagues across Advancement—including Major Gifts, Leadership Giving, Planned Giving, Alumni Engagement, Annual Giving, Prospect Research, and Advancement Services—to maximize donor engagement and philanthropic outcomes. Maintain accurate documentation of prospect activity, strategies, proposals, and next steps in the College's constituent database while ensuring timely follow-up and adherence to portfolio management standards. Serve as a mentor and strategic resource to members of the Major Gifts team by sharing principal gift fundraising strategies, participating in prospect strategy discussions, providing guidance on donor cultivation and solicitation approaches, and fostering the professional growth of frontline fundraisers through collaboration and knowledge sharing. Remain knowledgeable about institutional priorities, campaign initiatives, and charitable giving strategies in order to identify opportunities that align donor interests with College needs. Perform other related duties as assigned in support of Advancement strategy, campaign priorities, and institutional fundraising goals. The Associate Director of Principal Gifts operates in a highly relationship-driven and complex fundraising environment requiring independent judgment, strategic thinking, discretion, and exceptional interpersonal skills. The position develops customized strategies for sophisticated donors, balancing donor interests with institutional priorities while exercising considerable discretion in cultivation, solicitation timing, campus and leadership engagement, and the management of sensitive donor information. Working closely with Advancement leadership, colleagues, faculty, senior administrators, donors, volunteers, Trustees, and external advisors, the Associate Director manages a significant fundraising portfolio and coordinates institutional resources to cultivate, solicit, and steward principal gift prospects. While the position does not have direct supervisory responsibility, it frequently leads cross-functional teams and collaborative fundraising strategies, consulting with Advancement leadership on complex decisions as appropriate. Qualifications: Education : Bachelor's degree required; advanced degree or equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. Experience: Minimum of five years of progressively responsible experience in major gifts, principal gifts, planned giving, or related fundraising work. Experience in higher education fundraising is strongly preferred. Demonstrated success in cultivating, soliciting, and closing leadership-level and major and principal gift commitments, including complex and planned gift arrangements. Exceptional relationship-building, influencing, and communication skills, with the ability to present ideas persuasively, ask probing questions, listen carefully, and tailor messaging for highly sophisticated audiences. Strong strategic judgment, discretion, and political acumen in working with high-net-worth individuals, senior administrators, Trustees, and volunteer leaders. Experience managing portfolios, and programmatic workflows in a collaborative, goal-oriented fundraising environment. Ability to analyze donor capacity, motivation, timing, and institutional fit in order to develop effective moves-management strategies. Knowledge of charitable giving vehicles, donor stewardship principles, and the financial, tax, and ethical considerations relevant to major and principal gift fundraising. Cross-cultural competence and the ability to engage constituents with sensitivity across diverse backgrounds and, when needed, international contexts. Proficiency with fundraising databases, prospect tracking systems, standard office technologies, and written briefing/document preparation. Pay range: $105,000-$115,000 Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. No visa sponsorship is available for this position. EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3752": {
      "title": "Director of Equal Opportunity and Title IX",
      "description": "Category: Administration JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Director of Equal Opportunity serves as the College’s Title IX Coordinator, Title VI Coordinator and Section 504 Coordinator and leads the College’s equal opportunity, nondiscrimination and civil rights compliance efforts. The Director of Equal Opportunity will play a key role in continuing to shape the College’s vision of a single, central office responsible for integrating civil rights compliance with a trauma-informed, supportive culture of care. The Director will develop and administer policy, oversee the intake, investigation and resolution of complaints of discrimination and harassment involving students, faculty and staff, coordinate institution-wide training and prevention education, and foster an equitable, inclusive campus climate. This is a highly visible, pan-institutional role that will engage, collaborate and coordinate with various campus constituencies, including the Vice President of Strategic Planning and Institutional Diversity, Wyckoff Center Director, Campus Safety, Human Resources, Athletics, Residential Life, Campus Life and Engagement, Student Accessibility Services, The Learning Commons, academic leadership and the General Counsel, among others. Other key responsibilities include: Ensure all legally required training is delivered and, where regulations require, published publicly Train and functionally direct the deputy-coordinator network in Student Affairs, Human Resources, and the Dean of the Faculty’s office Oversee a single, centralized intake process for reports involving students, faculty and staff. Oversee the investigation, informal/alternative/restorative resolution and formal grievance processes, ensuring impartiality, timeliness and freedom from conflict or bias. Provide strategic direction for the College's Equal Opportunity and Title IX prevention programming. Steward partnerships with community engagement partners, including the Wyckoff Center, Office of Student Diversity and Inclusion, Human Resources, the Dean of Faculty’s office and Center for Leadership in Teaching and Learning, Student Accessibility Services, the Learning Commons, Health Promotion and the Counseling Center Own the integrity of the records-management system(s) for both student and employee matters. Coordinate with the Clery Act compliance function on VAWA-related crimes reporting, the Annual Security Report and timely warnings Convene and chair the multidisciplinary assessment and case-management team (Equal Opportunity Team) and serve on College-level shared governance committees as requested Ensure compliance with all annual compliance certifications, aggregate data reports, and periodic campus climate surveys Education : JD or Master’s Degree in higher education, law, human resources, social work or a related field required. Title IX Coordinator, investigator, hearing officer or civil rights investigator certification(s) preferred. Experience : Minimum 5 years of progressively responsible experience in Title IX administration, civil rights compliance, equal opportunity, HR/employee relations, higher education administration, law or investigations. Experience in a small liberal arts environment serving both student and employee matters preferred. Demonstrated working knowledge of federal and state laws related to unlawful discrimination and harassment, Section 504, Title IX, Clery, VAWA, Campus Sexual Violence Elimination Act (SaVE Act), NY State ‘Enough is Enough,” Title VII, Title VI and related state laws pertaining to prohibit unlawful discrimination and forms of interpersonal violence. Demonstrated ability to track and interpret rapidly changing laws, guidance and regulations. Experience developing and/or implementing civil rights policy and overseeing or delivering training to diverse audiences. Experience conducting or overseeing sensitive investigations and applying an evidentiary standard to reach sound, well-documented determinations. Knowledge of the dynamics of interpersonal violence, the impact of trauma interviewing, investigation techniques, and analyses of evidence to determine policy violations is preferred. Advanced written and verbal communications skills to work with diverse campus audiences at all levels of the institution, as well as proficiency with computers including Microsoft Office Suite. Familiarity with case-management systems and civil rights data reporting preferred. Pay range: $107,000 - $122,000 Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. No visa sponsorship is available for this position. EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3612": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Skidmore Fund",
      "description": "Category: Advancement JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Assistant/Associate Director, Skidmore Fund will be responsible for dollar growth, setting strategy and facilitating team efforts to secure leadership-level gifts and build a pipeline for major-gift level donors. Summary The Assistant/Associate Director, Skidmore Fund will be responsible for dollar growth, setting strategy and facilitating team efforts to secure leadership-level gifts and build a pipeline for major-gift level donors. Reporting to the Senior Director, Skidmore Fund, they manage the President’s Society (PS) and the Young Alumni President’s Society (YAPS) – leadership giving societies, in addition individually soliciting Skidmore Fund prospects. Duties include developing and implementing overall plans, setting metrics, partnering for meaningful donor stewardship, and helping to manage PS volunteers. Collaboration is critical as the Associate Director works closely with colleagues on building relationships and soliciting President's Society gifts. Finally, they develop relationships with alumni volunteers and prospective donors; identifying capacity, interests, and inclination, educating and cultivating these individuals, soliciting gifts, and stewarding donors and volunteers appropriately. Primary Job Duties The Assistant/Associate Director is responsible for overseeing Skidmore’s leadership giving program for the Skidmore Fund, including management of the overall program, coordination of an effort that involves multiple staff and programs within the Advancement team. Develops overall strategy and annual work plan, ensuring the PS and YAPS programs meet or exceed annual goals. Focus is on developing solicitation and stewardship plans, working with key volunteers, campus partners and working closely with colleagues in Advancement to increase leadership giving at Skidmore. Analyzes trends and best practices in leadership giving to strengthen and grow Skidmore’s efforts Responsible for developing philanthropic relationships with an assigned group of volunteers and prospects (75 ); identifying capacity, interests, and inclination, educating and cultivating these individuals, soliciting gifts, and stewarding donors and volunteers appropriately. Set goals and projections for overall dollars raised and participation from assigned pool Serves as primary contact for the Chair of the President's Society and partners with class managers to manage the relationship with class President's Society Chairs Ensures that all alumni scheduled for a President's Society ask are solicited as personally as possible. Assesses prospects not assigned to relationship managers for a short-term staff or volunteer assignment. Collaborates with Major Gifts team, Gift Planning, Alumni Relations and College Events, Parent and Family Giving, Donor Engagement and Stewardship, as well as Advancement Operations staff. Responsible for directly supporting fundraising in key reunion classes, including volunteer recruitment and management, developing relationships, goal setting (both dollars and donors), planning, prospecting, donor cultivation, and direct solicitation as necessary. Develop overall strategy and ensuring each class meets and surpasses individual donor and dollar goals. Focus is on managing class leadership, volunteers, and 18-month outreach efforts leading up to Reunion. Identify, recruit, and train class leadership volunteers and class agents. Demonstrate proficiency of planned giving options for appropriate classes. Develops and executes an annual giving effort focused on Skidmore’s faculty and staff, including a focus on direct marketing, stewardship, staff outreach, and recruitment of volunteers to help support this annual campaign. Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree required Experience: Fundraising within a higher ed or non-profit setting. Recruiting, developing, and motivating volunteers. Willingness to personally develop philanthropic relationships with prospective donors and volunteers. Skills/Knowledge/Abilities: Well-refined organization and project management skills, The ability to manage multiple projects and partners simultaneously and to work collaboratively with all fundraisers and other members of the Advancement staff, campus-wide partners, and large numbers of donors and volunteers. Strong written and communication skills, Ability to manage and adhere to deadlines. Understanding of the complexity and nuance of working with multiple volunteers of various experience levels. A valid driver's license is required A familiarity with technology and social networking platforms is also expected. If hired at the assistant level - minimum required experience 1-3 years. If hired at the associate level - minimum required experience 3-5 years. Salary range: Assistant level: $62,400 - $68,000 Associate level: $70,000 - $78,000 Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. No Visa Sponsorship is available for this position EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3634": {
      "title": "Psychiatric Consultant, Counseling Center",
      "description": "Category: Healthcare Support JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Skidmore College Counseling Center seeks a compassionate and collaborative Psychiatric Consultant (Psychiatrist or Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioner) to join our interdisciplinary mental health team. This is a part-time, 9-month position (3-4 hours per week for Fall and Spring Terms, so approximately 100 hours/total) providing psychiatric evaluation, medication management, and clinical consultation for undergraduate students. While we strongly prefer candidates who are able to provide services in person at the Counseling Center, we are also open to qualified candidates who wish to provide services virtually through a secure telehealth platform. Summary The Skidmore College Counseling Center seeks a compassionate and collaborative Psychiatric Consultant (Psychiatrist or Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioner) to join our interdisciplinary mental health team. This is a part-time, 9-month position (3-4 hours per week for Fall and Spring Terms, so approximately 100 hours/total) providing psychiatric evaluation, medication management, and clinical consultation for undergraduate students. While we strongly prefer candidates who are able to provide services in person at the Counseling Center, we are also open to qualified candidates who wish to provide services virtually through a secure telehealth platform. The Psychiatric Consultant works closely with Counseling Center clinicians to coordinate care for students receiving ongoing counseling services. Responsibilities include providing psychiatric evaluations, psychopharmacological assessment and treatment, medication management, participation in interdisciplinary treatment planning, and consultation to Counseling Center staff to support high-quality, student-centered care. The Counseling Center provides a broad range of services to the Skidmore community, including crisis intervention, brief individual counseling, group counseling, consultation, outreach programming, and after-hours crisis support. Students present with a wide range of developmental and psychiatric concerns, including anxiety, depression, trauma-related disorders, ADHD, eating disorders, substance use concerns, mood disorders, and other complex mental health needs. We are committed to providing timely, effective, inclusive, and culturally responsive care to all students. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellent diagnostic and psychopharmacology skills, strong written and verbal communication, the ability to collaborate effectively within a multidisciplinary treatment team, and a commitment to providing compassionate care to a diverse population of college students. They must provide proof of current NY state licensure and have the ability to prescribe medication in New York. Minimum Qualifications: MD or DO with Board Certification (or Board Eligibility) in Psychiatry or certification as a Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioner (PMHNP) Current New York State licensure Current DEA registration and prescribing authority (or ability to obtain prior to employment) Professional liability insurance Experience providing psychiatric evaluation and medication management Preferred Qualifications Experience working with traditional college-aged young adults Experience in a college counseling center, university health service, or outpatient mental health setting Experience working collaboratively within an interdisciplinary behavioral health team Demonstrated commitment to culturally responsive and inclusive mental health care Position Details Part-time, approximately 3–4 hours per week during Fall and Spring Terms (September-May) Flexible scheduling by mutual agreement Pay rate: $17,000.00 Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3620": {
      "title": "Tang Guide, Tang Teaching Museum and Art Gallery",
      "description": "Category: Office and Administrative Support JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Tang Guides program is a program that re-envisions visitor services at the museum. Tang Guides work alongside our students and full-time staff at our Visitor Services Desk, while also functioning as gallery ambassadors and tour guides. Tang Guides are an integral part of our front-line visitor services team, ensuring positive experiences for all patrons. This is a part time position. Summary The Tang Guides program is a program that re-envisions visitor services at the museum. Tang Guides work alongside our students and full-time staff at our Visitor Services Desk, while also functioning as gallery ambassadors and tour guides. Tang Guides are an integral part of our front-line visitor services team, ensuring positive experiences for all patrons. This is a part time position. Primary Job Duties: • Staffing the museum’s Visitor Services Desk: Tang Guides answer the main museum phone line, check in visitors to the museum, and perform other administrative tasks as assigned • Serving as roaming docents in the galleries: Tang Guides provide visitors with additional information about the Tang and specific exhibitions as they circulate through the galleries • Providing security in the exhibitions: Tang Guides maintain the safety of the artwork on display and ensure visitors follow both museum and exhibition requirements • Offering tours as requested: Tang Guides lead tours of the museum to individuals and groups who request them • Attending regular training workshops and tours, both in-person and via Zoom Tang Guides must be available to work evenings and weekends with regularity. There will be paid on-the-job training for this position which includes proper museum protocols/procedures, instruction on how to give tours, and on ways to connect with visitors. Tang Guides will also have opportunities to interact with curators and artists to gain greater insight into Tang exhibitions. A flexible schedule is key: in this position, there will be periods throughout the year with a significant number of workable hours available, and other times with fewer hours. Qualifications: This position requires a personable, organized, reliable, and task-oriented individual. Knowledge and appreciation of art/art history, especially contemporary art, is helpful. Past customer service or receptionist/front desk experience a plus. This position requires long periods of standing. Pay Rate: $17.54/hour Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3591": {
      "title": "Animal Caretaker",
      "description": "Category: Academic Support JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States Summary: The Animal Care Technician is a critical participant in the maintenance and management plan for the college’s census of laboratory animals used both in research and teaching. The successful candidate will provide support in the Animal Research Facility that includes daily care and maintenance of housing for rodents, several aquatic species, and amphibians that support research and teaching in several academic departments. This is a part-time “on-call” position, anticipated to average 10 hours per week, which may include weekend and holiday hours. Minimum Qualifications: high school diploma; demonstrated high level of reliability, responsibility, and attention to detail; experience working with animals (research, farm, or companion) beyond pet ownership; job requires lifting up to 50lbs, and ability to do tasks that require repetitive bending and reaching. Preferred Qualifications : Associate's degree or higher in a field related to animal care; experience working with laboratory animal research and technical animal facility support Pay rate: $18.50 per hour Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3062": {
      "title": "Per Diem Registered Nurse (RN) - As-Needed Position",
      "description": "Category: Healthcare Practitioner & Technical JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States Skidmore College is accepting applications for a PER DIEM Registered Nurse (RN) position (during business hours Monday-Friday, 9-5) in our Health Services department. Skidmore College is accepting applications for a per diem Registered Nurse (RN) position (in-person daytime hours only: Monday–Friday, 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m.; no nights, weekends, or holidays) in our Health Services department. Summary: Join Skidmore College Health Services as a Per Diem Registered Nurse. This as-needed position offers flexible, daytime-only hours (Monday–Friday, 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m.) — no nights, weekends, or holidays. The ideal candidate is available at least one weekday per week and is excited to collaborate with our Health Services team to provide high-quality, compassionate care to our students. Primary Job Duties: Takes a health history, conducts an appropriate assessment based on the presenting problems, evaluates findings and documents these clearly and succinctly in an electronic health record Differentiates between normal and abnormal findings Consults with Health Services’ clinicians to perform appropriate laboratory and other diagnostic tests, including rapid point-of-care tests and phlebotomy. Recognizes and empathizes with the unique psychological needs of the college-aged population and respects their right to privacy Recognizes when referral to a Health Services’ provider or community resource is appropriate, including utilization of appropriate clinical triage skills Offers appropriate treatments and education to students using Health Services’ protocols and practicing within the scope of their professional license Serves as a resource to the greater Skidmore College community regarding health issues, federal and state mandates and current medical practice Qualifications: Education : Current license to practice as a Registered Nurse in New York State Experience : 2-3 year minimum experience as a RN, college health experience highly desirable, ambulatory or Med/Surg experience valuable Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Pay Range: $30.00 - $34.00 per hour EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3081": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Teacher, Greenberg Child Care Center",
      "description": "Category: Child Care JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Greenberg Child Care Center at Skidmore College invites applications for a full-time 12-month Early Childhood Teacher position working with children ages 6 weeks to 5 years of age. The successful candidate must have excellent interpersonal skills, including the ability to work with college student teacher aides, as well as the ability to work with people of diverse backgrounds. The person must have knowledge and understanding of early childhood development and developmentally appropriate planning. The person must be able to lift and carry at least 30 pounds, be able to walk and stand for 7 hours a day, pass a physical exam and a comprehensive background check. They must complete 30 hours of training in child care and development topics every 2 years (15 hours must be completed during the first 6 months of employment). The Greenberg Child Care Center at Skidmore College invites applications for a full-time 12-month Early Childhood Teacher position working with children ages 6 weeks to 5 years of age in and energetic, physically active environment. Responsibilities: Supervision of children and providing a safe environment. Provide for the emotional, social, mental and physical needs of the children in care by planning cooperatively with co-workers to develop and carry out a developmentally appropriate curriculum. Provide care and nurturing to all children to create an environment where children feel safe, happy and free to explore and learn. Work cooperatively with co-workers to maintain a clean and organized classroom as well as maintaining other areas throughout the center and the playground. Updating required paperwork, some daily such as attendance records, daily health checks and anecdotal records, weekly plans, incident/accident reports and sunscreen and topical ointment permissions as needed. Personal training documentation must be updated as trainings are completed. Complete 30 hours of OCFS required training every 2 years in 10 topic areas. 15 hours must be completed and documented within the first 6 months of employment. Supervision and guidance of staff members including new teachers, assistant teachers, substitute teachers, college student employees and observers and volunteers, making sure all are following policies and procedures to meet regulation requirements. Qualifications: The successful candidate must have excellent interpersonal skills, including the ability to work with college student teacher aides, as well as the ability to work with people of diverse backgrounds. The person must have knowledge and understanding of early childhood development and developmentally appropriate planning. The person must be able to lift and carry at least 30 pounds, pass a physical exam and be able to walk and stand reach and grasp for 7 hours a day. The person must pass a comprehensive background check required by NYS OCFS. Must complete 30 hours of training in child care and development topics every 2 years (15 hours must be completed during the first 6 months of employment). Education Minimum of: Associates Degree in Early Childhood Education or a closely related field including early childhood development course work or a Child Development Associate (CDA) credential. Pay Range: $20.00 - $22.00 per hour commensurate with experience. Additional Benefits: Skidmore provides a generous benefits package including: Generous 4 weeks vacation (pro-rated the first year) Paid Holidays plus extra time during the Christmas and New Year's break Low cost-sharing for our healthcare benefits Low cost-sharing dental for the employee 1% contribution for the purpose of post-retirement healthcare Employer paid retirement contribution of 10% once eligible with no match by employee required (possibly eligible right away depending upon if you have participated in a 403(b) like Skidmore's at a previous higher ed institution) Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover letter Resume List of three references EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/3299": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Major Gifts",
      "description": "Category: Advancement JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United States The Assistant/Associate Director, Major Gifts is responsible for driving revenue growth, setting strategy, and supporting team efforts to secure major gifts while building a strong pipeline of donors. Reporting to the Executive Director of Major Gifts, the Assistant/Associate Director manages a portfolio of approximately 100+ prospects and donors, including alumni, parents, families, and friends of Skidmore College. This role focuses on soliciting gifts of $50,000 and higher and recruiting and managing fundraising volunteers. The Assistant/Associate Director partners with Advancement Research to identify and qualify new prospects, oversees solicitation strategies for annual and non-annual gifts, and develops individualized stewardship plans. The position works closely with Advancement colleagues, volunteers, administrators, and faculty across campus to advance institutional priorities and philanthropic goals. Summary The Assistant/Associate Director, Major Gifts will be responsible for dollar growth, setting strategy and facilitating team efforts to secure major gift-level gifts and build a pipeline of major-gift level donors. Reporting to the Executive Director of Major Gifts, the primary responsibilities for the Assistant/Associate Director, Major Gifts (ADMG) include developing long term philanthropic relationships with an assigned group of approximately 100+ prospects and donors, most of whom are alumni, friends, and family or parents of current Skidmore students. Responsible for solicitation of gifts with emphasis on those sized $50,000 and higher; and recruiting/managing fundraising volunteers. The ADMG will work with the Research Department to identify new prospects, oversee ask amounts for annual and non-annual gifts for their assignments and will be responsible for setting up individual stewardship plans for their donors. These responsibilities entail not only working with the Advancement staff but also with volunteers and administrators and faculty across campus. Primary Job Duties Maintain and execute appropriate strategies, from identification to stewardship, for each prospect/donor in assigned portfolio. Conduct asks of $50,000+ to assist Advancement in reaching revenue goal of raising approximately $25M per year. Plan and execute extensive travel to build and maintain philanthropic relationships with constituents. Conduct effective meetings either in-person or virtually with prospects/donors. Record prospect/donor interaction and strategy in database effectively. Demonstrate the ability to pull reports and manipulate data. Facilitate interactions between prospects/donors and key administrators, faculty, and volunteers. Relay key information between Skidmore and constituents. Collaborate with fellow Advancement staff, especially as their work pertains to prospect management, cultivation, stewardship, and strategy related to major gifts. Serve on Advancement-related projects as needed/assigned. Qualifications and Competencies Education : Bachelor’s Degree Experience : Requires three to five years of experience in major gift fundraising Fundraising (major gift and annual fund expertise with experience in facilities fundraising and working knowledge of planned giving), strong interpersonal and volunteer management skills, public speaking and writing, time management, judgment and decision making, and strong computer skills. This position requires a professional with a background characterized by initiative, leadership, creative energy, refined organizational skills, and proven fundraising expertise. The ADMG must be a superb communicator -- both written and oral -- who builds relationships and works well with varied constituents, including alumni, parents, friends, trustees, and staff. The ADMG must appreciate the important traditions of higher education and be able to articulate the mission and needs of the College, including the integral role of philanthropy in sustaining excellence and advancing strategic priorities. They must be able to make independent decisions, manage projects, work productively with colleagues and independent-minded volunteers, and inspire confidence among associates. Discretion and the ability to maintain confidences and confidential material are a must. Must be willing to relocate. This is not a remote position. The position requires travel 50% of the time or conduct virtual visits. Salary range: $65,000 - $85,000 Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable post-offer background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/2791": {
      "title": "On-Call Campus Safety Officer",
      "description": "Category: Safety and Security JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States Per-diem, generally overnight shift position available to NYS certified security guards or those able to be licensed (or meet law enforcement exemption) within 30 days of employment, and possess a valid driver’s license. Skidmore is seeking candidates generally for the Midnight to 8:00 AM Shift, including some weekends and holidays. When selecting applicants, priority will be given to those who can meet that specific schedule. Position is responsible for ensuring the safety of the campus community in a customer service centric manner. Campus Safety Officers conduct safety training, patrol, community outreach, report writing, emergency response, render first-aid, investigate incidents, document violations of law or policy and interacting with a diverse population. Involves vehicle and foot patrol, periodic dispatching and desk duties. There will be a background check. Prior applicants are encouraged to reapply. SUMMARY: Per-diem, generally overnight shift position available to NYS certified security guards or those able to be licensed (or meet law enforcement exemption) within 30 days of employment, and possess a valid driver’s license. Skidmore is seeking candidates generally for the Midnight to 8:00 AM Shift, including some weekends and holidays. When selecting applicants, priority will be given to those who can meet that specific schedule. Position is responsible for ensuring the safety of the campus community in a customer service centric manner. Campus Safety Officers conduct safety training, patrol, community outreach, report writing, emergency response, render first-aid, investigate incidents, document violations of law or policy and interacting with a diverse population. Involves vehicle and foot patrol, periodic dispatching and desk duties. Responsibilities include campus security patrol, report writing and enforcing college’s rules and regulations, conducting investigations and interacting with a diverse population. There will be a background check. Prior applicants may reapply. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Minimum qualifications include a high school diploma. Familiarity with computer applications is essential. At least one year of related experience is preferred but not required. A valid driver's license and a valid New York State Security Guard license at time of hire or within 30 days are required. Requirement may be waived for active or retired law enforcement. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Candidate should possess excellent communication and writing skills. PAY RANGE: $20.50 - $21.50 per hour DOCUMENTS NEEDED TO APPLY: To be considered for this position, please fill out an online application and attached the following documents: cover letter, resume and list of three references to include name, title, company, email address and telephone number. EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
    },
    "https://eodq.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX/job/2779": {
      "title": "Athletics Building Supervisor/Front Desk Monitor",
      "description": "Category: Office and Administrative Support JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Saratoga Springs, NY, United States The Athletics Building Supervisor / Front Desk Monitor is responsible for monitoring access to the building as well as ensuring a safe environment for those utilizing the facilities. Employee will be assigned to either the Williamson Sports Center or the McCaffery-Wagman Tennis and Wellness Center. The Building Supervisor / Front Desk Monitor will respond to emergencies within the building and will communicate directly with Campus Safety and the Department of Athletics, Fitness and Recreation staff as necessary regarding emergencies. The part-time Athletics Building Supervisor / Front Desk Monitor is responsible for monitoring access to the building as well as ensuring a safe environment for those utilizing the facilities. Employee will be assigned to either the Williamson Sports Center or the McCaffery-Wagman Tennis and Wellness Center. The Building Supervisor / Front Desk Monitor will respond to emergencies within the building and will communicate directly with Campus Safety and the Department of Athletics, Fitness and Recreation staff as necessary regarding emergencies. Job Responsibilities: Responsible for providing security and ensuring a safe environment for those utilizing the facilities within the Williamson Sports Center and McCaffery-Wagman Tennis and Wellness Center Unlocking/locking of both buildings and the outdoor tennis courts depending on assigned shift Assist with event set-up and break-down as assigned and provide oversight for student-workers assisting with the set-up or break-down of events, including those assigned to the cardio/weight rooms Provide general knowledge of events scheduled within the buildings Some clerical tasks as assigned Employee must be able to interact effectively with staff from the Department of Athletics, Fitness and Recreation, Counseling Center, Health Services and Health Promotions. Employee must engage users of the facility and direct users to appropriate spaces within the buildings. Interact closely with athletic staff, students, faculty, tour groups, visiting teams and visitors to campus (facility rentals). Will act as a liaison with Campus Safety. Employee must have the maturity to be able to make judgement calls regarding a variety of potential building situations (e.g. asking someone to leave the premises if misusing the facilities). Must have excellent interpersonal skills and be able to defuse potentially volatile situations that may arise and know when to alert Campus Safety to assist with situation. Education : High School Diploma required, Associates or Bachelor’s Degree preferred Experience : High degree of reliability High degree of maturity with sound judgement and decision-making skills Excellent interpersonal skills with friendly demeanor Ability to work morning, evening and/or weekend hours Typical work includes walking around the building and some light lifting when assignment includes a set-up or break-down Pay rate: $16.00 per hour Required documents needed to apply: On-line application Cover Letter Resume List of Three References EEO STATEMENT Skidmore College is committed to being an inclusive campus community and, as an Equal Opportunity Employer, does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, gender, age, national or ethnic origin, physical or mental disability, military or veteran status, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, predisposition or carrier status, domestic violence victim status, familial status, dating violence, or stalking, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state or local laws. Employment at Skidmore College is contingent upon an acceptable background check result. CREATIVE THOUGHT MATTERS. "
